      {
        "id": 200938,
        "name": "MindWanderer",
        "username": "MindWanderer",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mindwanderer/{size}/4816_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-08-03T18:17:30.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Powerhound_2000\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/powerhound_2000/48/1859_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Powerhound_2000:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I thought what defined a Relic in the game was interesting as it had bothered me that Instruments weren't Relics but were an intricate part of his character. &nbsp; The idea that a Relic has power to it that whomever uses it will be granted that power whereas his Instruments while powerful do not impart powers upon just anyone. &nbsp;Makes me wonder about the items in Apostate's deck and if there were other wielders of it along with what granted them power since he is directly tied to them. &nbsp;Also, what would happen if someone took Fanatic's Aegis and Sword along with how powerful Citizen Truth's shield is to stop a Relic. &nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I don't think they've been consistent about that. &nbsp;We know that Absolution, for instance, only has power because Fanatic believes it should. &nbsp;I don't think it would work for anything else. &nbsp;But they also suggested that the \"Relic\" keyword is more about how resistant to destruction the item in question is, and we know the instruments get destroyed all the time.</p>",
